// Punar: the person at the keyboard may restart and shut down their own
// machine, without being asked for a password first.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The session menu's Restart row did nothing on a real
// device, and the reason was two layers below the button. `systemctl reboot`
// is a polkit request, and systemd ships
// org.freedesktop.login1.reboot-multiple-sessions as auth_admin_keep even for
// an active local session. So the moment a machine has a second live session —
// a lingering greeter, a second VT, a `systemd-run --machine` shell — logind
// answers "challenge" instead of "yes", and the reboot silently does not
// happen unless an authentication agent is running to raise a dialog.
//
// THE DECISION. On a Punar desktop the admitted `punar` member is the person
// who owns the machine; the power button on the case restarts it with no
// password at all, and requiring one here buys nothing against an attacker who
// already has physical access. macOS's Apple menu behaves exactly this way,
// and this menu is modelled on it.
//
// THE THREE NARROWINGS THAT KEEP THIS HONEST:
//   * `subject.local && subject.active` — only the seat that is actually in
//     front of the machine. A remote or background session gets the shipped
//     behaviour, prompt and all.
//   * `punar` group membership — the admission group every human account on
//     this device joins, and nothing else does.
//   * The `-ignore-inhibit` actions are DELIBERATELY ABSENT. An application
//     holding a shutdown inhibitor ("still writing your file") must still be
//     able to say so; overriding that is a different decision from "may this
//     person reboot", and it is not one this file makes.
//
// WITH ANOTHER SESSION OPEN, ONLY A DEVICE ADMINISTRATOR (F0-S1, 2026-09-25;
// docs/api/ipc.md section 23.2). The `-multiple-sessions` actions are the ones
// logind asks about when someone else is signed in too, and restarting then
// ends that person's session and whatever they had not saved — an action that
// reaches another person. So those two need the `punar-admin` role, which
// polkit reads the way a login would (getgrouplist through nss-systemd, so a
// role taken away is gone at the next question). Anyone else is told no
// outright rather than challenged for a password nobody can type here, and
// `punarctl session restart` says it was refused. A restart with no other
// session open reaches nobody and stays open to the person at the seat.
// Two limits, stated: polkit cannot spend a punar-authd ticket, so the seat's
// physical presence (local and active) stands in for a fresh password here;
// and it reads the device's own group, not an organization's pinned list.
//
// Everything else falls through to the shipped policy: NOT_HANDLED means "no
// opinion", not "yes".
polkit.addRule(function (action, subject) {
    if (!subject.local || !subject.active) {
        return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
    }
    if (!subject.isInGroup("punar")) {
        return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
    }
    switch (action.id) {
    case "org.freedesktop.login1.reboot":
    case "org.freedesktop.login1.power-off":
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    case "org.freedesktop.login1.reboot-multiple-sessions":
    case "org.freedesktop.login1.power-off-multiple-sessions":
        return subject.isInGroup("punar-admin") ? polkit.Result.YES : polkit.Result.NO;
    }
    return polkit.Result.NOT_HANDLED;
});
